his  obviously makes sense for a number of  reasons including what summer said in  this interview Mysterio is a villain of  deception and illusions in the comics he  is constantly trying to trick Peter  Parker and spider-man and ultimately it  made the most sense to see him as he was  in the movie a villain trying to trick  everybody into thinking that he is a  hero  the writers then talked about the ending  and the mid-credits scene and said that  it was always a plan to reveal Peters  identity to the world  however they originally had him reveal  his identity in the final battle and not  the mid-credits scene and the interview  McKenna revealed we played with the idea  peter is the one who sacrifices his  identity out of necessity during the  final battle then it seemed more  interesting if Mysterio tricks him into  doing it but anytime we wrote a version  where he was being revealed to the world  in that battle it felt like it  diminished the victory so before it  became a tag it was really just the end  of the movie right as he feels he's  stepping up a spider-man he has the rug  pulled out from under him again so the  writers go on to talk about why they  wanted to reveal spider-man's identity  to the world and how much of an effect  it would have on future spider-man films  in the interview McKenna said we were  challenged by the producers to come up  with something that Peter sacrifices by  the end of the movie and when we hit  upon that as a group it became a very  scary idea oh no we can't do that then  it's not a spider-man movie anymore  it's such a bold maneuver that it became  inevitable particularly with a tricky  character like Mysterio who's this dark  father figure from the grave is he  trying to give Peter his I am Iron Man  moment it's thrust upon him but is this  a lesson or a punishment Sommers then  commented we were definitely debating  should we just reveal who's spider-man  is or should we frame him for something  and turn him into a pariah 